        astrophotography: "A few words about astrophotography - what problem does a star tracker solve?",
        astrophotographyVal1: "Photos of celestial objects are actually the result of a process called stacking - combining multiple images into one. This improves the signal-to-noise ratio and overall image quality. Capturing a full series of images can take an entire night. When shooting from a regular tripod, the object being photographed would quickly drift out of the frame. This happens because the stars appear to continuously move across the sky around the celestial pole. The job of a star tracker is to keep the object in the frame for many hours. To achieve this, the camera must continuously - albeit very slowly - change its position relative to the Earth.",
        astrophotographyVal2: "A star tracker is a motorized equatorial mount. To do its job properly, it must be positioned in a very specific way relative to the Earth. The axis around which the mount rotates, known as the right ascension (RA) axis, must be parallel to the Earth's axis - pointing north (or south, depending on the hemisphere) and tilted at an angle equal to the observer's latitude. If the tracker is not aligned correctly, the resulting images will appear blurred, and the object being photographed will eventually drift out of the field of view.",
