        subnets: {
          header: "Subnets",
          value: "The home network included several computers, printers, and access points. I created several VLANs on the MikroTik - separate networks for servers, home devices, and guests - so that the failure or compromise of one device would not automatically expose the rest of the network. This required configuring the Cisco switch accordingly and creating firewall rules to restrict access between the networks.",
        },
        backupIsp: {
          header: "Setting up a backup internet connection",
          value1: "My experience in the previous apartment made me want to have a backup ISP in addition to a UPS. Moreover, I wanted it to be independent of the building's power grid, so that I could still access the internet during a power outage. I opted for a mobile internet connection - bought a USB modem and connected it to the router.",
          value2: "The real challenge turned out to be the configuration - I had two ISPs, meaning two different (and dynamic) IP addresses. On top of that, the mobile connection gave me no way to configure things like port forwarding. To solve this problem, I rented a VPS, set up a WireGuard VPN server on it, and established a connection to my server room. I pointed my domains to the VPS's public IP address and configured it to forward all incoming traffic through the tunnel. I also had to make several changes to my router's configuration - all traffic coming from the tunnel had to be properly marked so that the response packets would also be routed back through the tunnel. I additionally wrote a script that pinged the VPS through the primary ISP interface - if the ping failed, it changed the default route to use the mobile connection instead.",
        },
